#!/bin/sh
# paseo-agent-shim — the AGENT-NEUTRAL half of this kit's entrypoint behavior.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: the kit becomes the sandbox entrypoint by dropping a
# wrapper at ~/.local/bin/<agent> that SHADOWS the real agent binary (the base
# image runs the bare, unqualified agent command, and ~/.local/bin is first on
# PATH). That per-agent wrapper is deliberately THIN — it only (a) passes real
# invocations through to the real agent binary, and (b) on the bare no-arg
# entrypoint path, hands off to THIS shim. All the Paseo/kit-specific work lives
# here and knows NOTHING about any particular agent, so a second agent is
# supported by dropping another thin wrapper that execs this same shim
# (see docs/decisions/paseo-mixin-generic-wrapper.md).
#
# INVOCATION CONTRACT: the thin wrapper must `exec` this shim (not run it as a
# child) on the no-arg path. That way the shim INHERITS the wrapper's process id —
# so when the wrapper was the sandbox entrypoint, the shim is PID 1, and the
# PID-1 hold below actually keeps the sandbox alive. Running it as a child would
# give the shim a non-1 pid and the hold would never trigger.
#
# WHAT THE SHIM DOES on the no-arg entrypoint path:
#   1. PIN PASEO'S WORKTREE ROOT to the current project. Paseo keeps agent git
#      worktrees under a SINGLE GLOBAL root; there is no per-project option, and a
#      relative root resolves against PASEO_HOME. On `acq run <path>` the entrypoint
#      runs with cwd = the primary (first mounted) workspace, so the shim computes
#      <PWD>/.paseo-worktrees and writes it to $PASEO_HOME/config.json (via
#      paseo-set-worktrees-root.mjs). The daemon reads worktrees.root at worker
#      start, so when the value CHANGES the shim restarts the daemon worker via
#      `paseo daemon restart` (falling back to a marker-scoped process kill only
#      if the CLI restart cannot run).
#   2. PRINT HOST-CONNECT INSTRUCTIONS and, when it IS PID 1 (the `acq run`
#      entrypoint), HOLD the foreground so that session's sandbox — and thus the
#      daemon/UI — stays up.
#
# The primary-workspace path comes from the shim's OWN cwd, which equals the
# primary (first mounted) workspace on every acq backend (the agent entrypoint is
# launched there). The shim needs no marker file or env var for this. (Note: the
# guest marker /var/lib/acq/workspace exists on some backends but not all, so it is
# NOT a portable signal — cwd is.)
#
# WHY THE ENTRYPOINT MUST NOT RETURN WHEN IT IS PID 1 (keep the sandbox alive):
#   A Docker sandbox is "running" only while its entrypoint / PID 1 is alive. On
#   the `acq run` path the shim (exec'd from the wrapper) IS PID 1; if it returns,
#   PID 1 exits and the sandbox transitions running -> stopped (taking the Paseo
#   daemon/UI with it). So when `$$` is 1 the shim ends by BLOCKING FOREVER in
#   hold_pid1. On a detached `acq create` PID 1 is instead a tini keepalive shim
#   and this shim is non-PID-1 only if invoked directly. The opencode wrapper
#   sends bare shell / `acq exec` calls to the real TUI instead.
#
# The Paseo daemon is UNSECURED (no PASEO_PASSWORD): the sandbox — an ephemeral
# container with proxied, allow-listed egress and no host FS — is the security
# boundary, and its published port is host loopback only. See the README
# "Security" section.

set -eu

. "${HOME:-/home/agent}/paseo-timing.sh"
paseo_timing shim_begin

# Daemon bind. Keep in sync with the kit spec's PASEO_LISTEN / publishedPorts.
PASEO_PORT="$(printf '%s' "${PASEO_LISTEN:-0.0.0.0:6767}" | sed -n 's/.*:\([0-9][0-9]*\)$/\1/p')"
[ -n "$PASEO_PORT" ] || PASEO_PORT=6767

HOME_DIR="${HOME:-/home/agent}"
PASEO_HOME_DIR="${PASEO_HOME:-$HOME_DIR/.paseo}"
STATE_DIR="${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME_DIR/.local/state}/paseo"
DAEMON_LOG="$STATE_DIR/paseo-daemon.log"
SET_ROOT_SCRIPT="$HOME_DIR/paseo-set-worktrees-root.mjs"

# 1. Pin Paseo's worktree root to <PWD>/.paseo-worktrees. PWD here is the primary
#    (first mounted) workspace on the `acq run` path. Use an ABSOLUTE path (Paseo
#    resolves a relative root against PASEO_HOME, not the project).
WORKTREES_ROOT="$(pwd -P)/.paseo-worktrees"
worktrees_changed=""
paseo_timing root_begin
if [ -f "$SET_ROOT_SCRIPT" ] && command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # The helper prints "changed" / "unchanged" on success, or "error: ..." + rc=1.
  _set_out="$(PASEO_HOME="$PASEO_HOME_DIR" node "$SET_ROOT_SCRIPT" --root "$WORKTREES_ROOT" 2>&1 || true)"
  case "$_set_out" in
    changed)   worktrees_changed=yes; paseo_timing root_changed ;;
    unchanged) paseo_timing root_unchanged ;;
    *)         paseo_timing root_error 1; echo "paseo: could not pin Paseo worktree root: $_set_out" >&2 ;;
  esac
fi
paseo_timing root_end

# 2. If the root changed, restart the daemon worker so it re-reads config.json.
#    Paseo 0.9 exposes `paseo daemon restart` for this. If the daemon is wedged or
#    the CLI surface changes again, fall back to killing ONLY the supervised Paseo
#    tree, never the startup script's supervisor loop. The supervisor loop's argv
#    carries the marker "supervisor:paseo-daemon"; the actual daemon child is a
#    `paseo daemon run` tree beneath that loop.
daemon_up() {
  # /api/health is unauthenticated and returns 200 when the daemon is listening.
  curl -fsS "http://127.0.0.1:$PASEO_PORT/api/health" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# ui_ready: is the BROWSER UI fully serveable, not just the API? This is stronger
# than daemon_up() on purpose. The web UI ships content-hashed assets served with
# `Cache-Control: immutable`, so if a browser loads the page during the boot
# window — after /api/health answers but before the daemon is actually serving the
# hashed JS bundle intact — Chrome can cache a bad/partial response FOREVER
# (immutable), and every later reload black-pages with ERR_CONTENT_DECODING_FAILED
# until the cache is cleared. To avoid steering users to open the browser too
# early, we confirm the entry bundle referenced by index.html downloads COMPLETE.
#
# The bundle is served CHUNKED (no Content-Length), so we can't compare against a
# response header. Instead we compare the bytes actually received on the wire to
# the size of the daemon's own on-disk precompressed artifact (the .br file it
# serves for `Accept-Encoding: br`, what a browser sends). curl codec support is
# irrelevant — we never decode, only count bytes. Returns 0 only when the full
# body is delivered.
# Resolve the kit's per-user installation first, then the inherited PATH.
# Startup normally prepends the per-user bin only in its own process tree;
# attach does not inherit that PATH change. Keep this lookup local to artifacts.
# Prefer its nested dependency; only use a same-prefix hoisted server when no
# nested server exists. Unknown layouts retain the original search below.
paseo_bundle_artifact() {
  _artifact_bin="$HOME_DIR/.npm-global/bin/paseo"
  if [ ! -f "$_artifact_bin" ] || [ ! -x "$_artifact_bin" ]; then
    _artifact_bin="$(command -v paseo 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  fi
  _artifact_bin="$(readlink -f "$_artifact_bin" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  case "$_artifact_bin" in
    */node_modules/@getpaseo/cli/bin/paseo) ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  _artifact_cli="${_artifact_bin%/bin/paseo}"
  _artifact_server="$_artifact_cli/node_modules/@getpaseo/server"
  if [ ! -d "$_artifact_server" ]; then
    _artifact_server="${_artifact_cli%/cli}/server"
  fi
  _artifact_file="$_artifact_server/dist/server/web-ui$1.br"
  [ -f "$_artifact_file" ] && [ -r "$_artifact_file" ] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$_artifact_file"
}

ui_ready() {
  paseo_timing ui_health_begin
  _ui_rc=0
  daemon_up || _ui_rc=$?
  paseo_timing ui_health_end "$_ui_rc"
  [ "$_ui_rc" -eq 0 ] || return 1
  paseo_timing ui_index_begin
  _idx="$(curl -fsS "http://127.0.0.1:$PASEO_PORT/" 2>/dev/null)" || _ui_rc=$?
  paseo_timing ui_index_end "$_ui_rc"
  [ "$_ui_rc" -eq 0 ] || return 1
  _bundle="$(printf '%s' "$_idx" \
    | grep -oE '/_expo/static/js/web/index-[0-9a-f]+\.js' | head -1)"
  # No hashed bundle in index.html => can't assert; treat "root serves HTML" as
  # the best available readiness signal rather than blocking forever.
  [ -n "$_bundle" ] || return 0
  # Locate the on-disk .br the daemon would serve; its size is the expected wire
  # length for an Accept-Encoding: br fetch.
  paseo_timing ui_find_begin
  _brfile="$(paseo_bundle_artifact "$_bundle")" || \
    _brfile="$(find / -path "*/web-ui$_bundle.br" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
  paseo_timing ui_find_end
  paseo_timing ui_bundle_begin
  _got="$(curl -fsS -H 'Accept-Encoding: br' -o /dev/null \
      -w '%{size_download}' "http://127.0.0.1:$PASEO_PORT$_bundle" 2>/dev/null)" || _ui_rc=$?
  paseo_timing ui_bundle_end "$_ui_rc"
  [ "$_ui_rc" -eq 0 ] || return 1
  paseo_timing ui_size_begin
  if [ -n "$_brfile" ] && [ -r "$_brfile" ]; then
    _expected="$(wc -c < "$_brfile" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
    if [ -n "$_expected" ] && [ "$_got" = "$_expected" ]; then
      _ui_rc=0
    else
      _ui_rc=1
    fi
  else
    # Can't find the artifact to size-check against: fall back to "got a
    # non-trivial body" rather than blocking readiness forever.
    if [ "${_got:-0}" -gt 100000 ] 2>/dev/null; then
      _ui_rc=0
    else
      _ui_rc=1
    fi
  fi
  paseo_timing ui_size_end "$_ui_rc"
  return "$_ui_rc"
}
timed_ui_ready() {
  paseo_timing ui_probe_begin
  _probe_rc=0
  ui_ready || _probe_rc=$?
  paseo_timing ui_probe_end "$_probe_rc"
  return "$_probe_rc"
}
if [ -n "$worktrees_changed" ]; then
  echo "paseo: pinned Paseo worktree root to $WORKTREES_ROOT; restarting the daemon to apply it ..."
  paseo_timing restart_begin
  if command -v paseo >/dev/null 2>&1 && paseo daemon restart --home "$PASEO_HOME_DIR" --timeout 30 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    paseo_timing restart_end
  else
    paseo_timing restart_end "$?"
    paseo_timing fallback_begin
    echo "paseo: daemon restart command failed; falling back to supervisor respawn" >&2
    killed_daemon=""
    for _pid in $(pgrep -u "$(id -u)" -f "paseo daemon run" 2>/dev/null); do
      # Skip the supervisor loop (its cmdline carries the marker).
      if tr '\0' ' ' < "/proc/$_pid/cmdline" 2>/dev/null | grep -q "supervisor:paseo-daemon"; then
        continue
      fi
      if kill "$_pid" 2>/dev/null; then
        killed_daemon=yes
        paseo_timing fallback_kill_sent
      fi
    done
    [ -n "$killed_daemon" ] && rm -f "$PASEO_HOME_DIR/paseo.pid" 2>/dev/null || true
    paseo_timing fallback_end
  fi
fi

# Wait for initial startup or the restarted daemon with the same bounded budget.
# An early-seeded root avoids a restart, not the cold-start health allowance.
_i=0
paseo_timing health_wait_begin
_health_rc=1
while [ "$_i" -lt 30 ]; do
  paseo_timing health_probe_begin
  _health_rc=0
  daemon_up || _health_rc=$?
  paseo_timing health_probe_end "$_health_rc"
  [ "$_health_rc" -eq 0 ] && break
  _i=$((_i + 1))
  sleep 1
done
paseo_timing health_wait_end "$_health_rc"

# 3. Report status + how to reach it. Give the daemon a brief moment if it is
#    still coming up (first boot installs the CLI first), and — importantly — wait
#    until the browser UI is FULLY serveable, not just /api/health, before telling
#    the user to open it. Opening during the boot window can poison Chrome's
#    immutable asset cache (see ui_ready() above), which black-pages every later
#    reload until the cache is cleared.
ui_is_ready=""
paseo_timing ui_wait_begin
if timed_ui_ready; then
  ui_is_ready=yes
  echo "paseo: Paseo daemon + web UI are up on 127.0.0.1:$PASEO_PORT (started by the kit startup script)"
else
  # Wait for full UI readiness (bundle serveable), not just the API. First boot
  # also installs the CLI, so allow a generous window.
  _i=0
  while [ "$_i" -lt 60 ]; do
    if timed_ui_ready; then ui_is_ready=yes; break; fi
    _i=$((_i + 1))
    sleep 1
  done
  if [ -n "$ui_is_ready" ]; then
    echo "paseo: Paseo daemon + web UI are up on 127.0.0.1:$PASEO_PORT"
  elif daemon_up; then
    echo "paseo: daemon API is up on :$PASEO_PORT but the web UI bundle isn't fully" >&2
    echo "       serveable yet — WAIT for it to finish before opening the browser," >&2
    echo "       or you may cache a broken asset (hard-reload fixes it). See $DAEMON_LOG" >&2
  else
    echo "paseo: Paseo daemon not answering yet on :$PASEO_PORT; the startup" >&2
    echo "       supervisor keeps retrying — see $DAEMON_LOG" >&2
  fi
fi
if [ -n "$ui_is_ready" ]; then
  paseo_timing ui_wait_end
else
  paseo_timing ui_wait_end 1
fi

cat <<EOF

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Connect from your HOST
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
This sandbox publishes container port $PASEO_PORT (the Paseo daemon API,
WebSocket, and bundled web UI — all on one port) to a host loopback port. Find
the mapped host port from your host shell:

    acq ports <sandbox>

  • Paseo web UI → container $PASEO_PORT → open http://127.0.0.1:<host-port>

Paseo launches its own agent CLIs; there is no shared live session with a
terminal TUI, so drive agents from the browser UI.
EOF

if [ -n "$ui_is_ready" ]; then
  echo "The web UI is READY now — safe to open."
else
  cat <<'EOF'
The web UI is still coming up. WAIT until it finishes before opening the browser:
opening during startup can make the browser cache a broken bundle (a black page
that persists on reload). If that happens, do ONE hard reload with cache
disabled — DevTools ▸ Network ▸ "Disable cache", then ⌘/Ctrl-Shift-R — or open
the URL in a private/Incognito window once.
EOF
fi
cat <<'EOF'
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
EOF

# Are WE PID 1? This discriminates the two no-arg situations:
#   - PID 1 (we ARE the sandbox entrypoint, i.e. the `acq run` path): we MUST NOT
#     return, or PID 1 exits and the sandbox stops. Hold forever (hold_pid1).
#   - NOT PID 1 (direct shim invocation only; the opencode wrapper instead opens
#     the real TUI): the daemon + UI are already supervised by the startup
#     script, so there is nothing for us to hold — EXIT CLEANLY.
is_pid1() { [ "$$" -eq 1 ]; }
paseo_timing shim_end

if is_pid1; then
  cat <<EOF

NOTE: you started this with \`acq run\`, so THIS terminal is the sandbox's main
process. It will stay held open — keep this terminal open while you use Paseo
from your browser. Closing it stops the sandbox.

If you did NOT want a held terminal: quit this (Ctrl-C), then bring the sandbox
up detached instead — it keeps running on its own with no terminal attached:
    acq create --name <sandbox> <agent> <path>
    acq ports <sandbox>        # find the host port, then open the browser

Daemon log: $DAEMON_LOG
EOF

  hold_pid1() {
    # Block forever without spinning. Prefer an inert sleep loop; never return.
    while :; do
      sleep 86400
    done
  }

  hold_pid1
else
  cat <<EOF

The sandbox keeps running on its own; the Paseo daemon and web UI are supervised
by the kit startup script. This command is exiting and returning your terminal.
Reach Paseo from the host with:
    acq ports <sandbox>        # then open http://127.0.0.1:<host-port-for-$PASEO_PORT>

Daemon log: $DAEMON_LOG
EOF
  exit 0
fi
